<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atharvaveda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Atharva Veda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Atharvaveda is the fourth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, the sacred texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, compiled around 1000 BCE or somewhat later. Besides hymns, it contains spells and charms for healing, protection, love and harming enemies, as well as speculation on the cosmos, giving a picture of everyday religion beyond the great sacrifices. It mentions peoples such as the Angas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadhas</a> and praises King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parikshit">Parikshit</a> of the Kurus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atharvaveda.jpg" alt="Atharvaveda" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Dwight Whitney / Charles Rockwell Lanman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atharva-Veda_samhita_page_471_illustration.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Atharvaveda is a collection of 20 books containing 730 hymns of about 6,000 stanzas. Its language differs from that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> and preserves older Indo-European archaisms. About a sixth of the text adapts verses of the Rigveda, mainly from its tenth book, and the 20th book is almost entirely borrowed from it. Its oldest name, given in its own verse 10.7.20, is Atharvangirasah. Michael Witzel dates the hymns to the early Indian Iron Age, because they name iron as black metal; no Vedic text can be dated absolutely.</p><p>Early Indian tradition recognised only three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, and the early Buddhist Nikaya texts mention three. Acceptance of the Atharvaveda as the fourth probably came in the second half of the 1st millennium BCE. Its priests were counted among the lowest tier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, and a stigma against them is recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> into modern times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text was arranged by the length of its hymns, not by subject. Roughly, the first seven books hold charms for healing and sorcery, Books 8 to 12 speculate on a variety of topics, and Books 13 to 18 concern rites of passage. Many charms seek long life or recovery from illness, often with a plant or amulet, while others serve soldiers, lovers, farmers and households. Hymn 4.15 of the Paippalada edition deals with a fractured bone, and hymn 8.7 praises medicinal herbs. Diseases are taken to be caused by evil spirits.</p><p>It is sometimes called the Veda of magical formulas, a label many scholars reject. Jan Gonda points out that much of it is domestic ritual without magic, and some hymns are prayers for peace or speculation, such as hymn 10.7 on Skambha, the cosmic pillar, a synonym for Brahman. The text also includes a Brahmana, the Gopatha, and three principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>: the Mundaka, the Mandukya and the Prashna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Two recensions survive, the Shaunakiya and the Paippalada, the latter the more ancient. Reliable Paippalada manuscripts were thought lost until Durgamohan Bhattacharyya found palm-leaf manuscripts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> in 1957. Rudolf Roth and William Dwight Whitney published the Shaunakiya text in 1856, Ralph T.H. Griffith made the first complete English translation in 1895-96, and Whitney's nearly complete translation of 1905 is still cited.</p><p>The Caraka Samhita and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sushruta-samhita">Sushruta Samhita</a> direct physicians to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, especially the Atharvaveda, and Indian tradition links Ayurveda to it. Dominik Wujastyk notes, however, that the systematic medical literature begins with those two works. Verse 11.7.24 contains the oldest known mention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> as a genre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Atharvaveda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Atharvaveda</b> treats the Magadhas as outsiders to Vedic orthodoxy, and the Mahabharata preserves legends of an earlier Brihadratha dynasty and its king Jarasandha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Shruti, “what is heard”, is revealed knowledge received by the ancient seers: the four Vedas, the Rigveda, Samaveda, Yajurveda and <b>Atharvaveda</b>, each with its Brahmanas, ritual commentaries; Aranyakas, forest treatises; and Upanishads, philosophical dialogues.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Vedic people believed in the transmigration of the soul, and by the time of the <b>Atharvaveda</b> the peepul tree and the cow were sacred.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>It is mentioned in the <b>Atharvaveda</b> as a distant eastern people and by the 6th century BCE it was listed among the sixteen mahajanapadas.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-anga">Kingdom of Anga</a></li><li><blockquote>Under kings remembered in the <b>Atharvaveda</b> and the Brahmanas, notably Parikshit and his son Janamejaya, it became the dominant power of northern India, with centres at Asandivat and Hastinapura.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuru-kingdom">Kuru Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
